   "source": [
    "# CPMIP Skill–Energy Analysis\n",
    "**Created with assitance of Claude Sonnet version 5.0 by Paul Smith 23-07-2026 paul.smith@ext.esa.int**\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook explores how to visualise **scientific performance vs. computational/energy cost**\n",
    "using CPMIP-style data (as in the CMIP6 GMD paper table). It is written to work directly with a\n",
    "table in the same shape as `CPMIPs_for_CMIP6__GMD_paper_data_.xlsx` (sheet `CPMIP metrics`),\n",
    "but is structured so you can point it at any similarly-shaped dataset.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Three views are built up in turn:**\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Skill–energy diagrams** — skill/evaluation metric vs. an energy or cost metric, restricted to comparable model classes or experiment types.\n",
    "2. **Efficiency frontiers** — the Pareto-optimal configurations that get the most scientific performance per unit of energy/cost.\n",
    "3. **Energy-adjusted interpretation** — narrower questions about resolution, complexity, and cross-generation/platform comparisons.\n",
    "\n",
    "> **Important caveat on \"skill\":** the CPMIP table captures *computational* performance\n",
    "> (SYPD, CHSY, JPSY, resolution, complexity, memory bloat, etc.) but does **not** contain a\n",
    "> scientific skill/evaluation score (e.g. RMSE against observations, a PMP/ESMValTool metric,\n",
    "> a CMEC score). Section 0.3 below makes this explicit and gives you two options:\n",
    "> plug in your own skill metric (recommended), or use a clearly-flagged synthetic placeholder\n",
    "> so the plotting code can be demonstrated and tested end-to-end. **Never treat the placeholder\n",
    "> numbers as real scientific results.**\n"
   ]
  },
